/**
 * @module isolate
 *
 * Runs code in parallel on a pool of operating system threads.
 *
 * ## How isolates work
 *
 * Each isolate runs in a virtual machine of its own, with its own
 * garbage-collected heap, call stack and copy of every module it
 * imports. Isolates run at the same time on different CPU cores, but
 * they never share heap objects with each other or with the main
 * thread. The design is closer to Erlang processes and Dart isolates
 * than to threads that share memory.
 *
 * Values move between isolates by being copied. When you pass a value
 * to `spawn()`, return one from an isolate's function or send one
 * through a `Channel`, Zuri copies the whole object graph into the
 * receiving isolate's heap. The copy keeps cycles, objects that appear
 * more than once in the value, and the classes of instances.
 *
 * ## Tasks and the thread pool
 *
 * `isolate.spawn()` doesn't start a thread. It packages the function
 * and its arguments into a task, which is a copy that doesn't depend on
 * the caller's heap, and adds the task to a shared queue. The threads
 * in the pool take tasks from the queue and run them one after another,
 * each in a virtual machine that the thread keeps from one task to the
 * next.
 *
 * The pool has at most one thread for each CPU core, unless you set a
 * different limit with `configure()`. A thread is only started when a
 * task is waiting and every existing thread is busy, so the pool never
 * runs more threads than the work needs.
 *
 * Because a queued task is a copy of some values, not a suspended
 * thread, you can queue a large number of tasks at once, 100,000 or
 * more, without paying for a stack or a heap for each one. Each task
 * takes a few hundred bytes for its place in the queue and its handle,
 * plus whatever its arguments take up.
 *
 * ## Garbage collection
 *
 * Each isolate collects its own garbage, in its own nursery and old
 * generation, on its own thread. A collection in one isolate never
 * pauses another isolate or the main thread. That's why
 * allocation-heavy work, such as building trees, multiplying matrices
 * or rendering images in parallel, speeds up almost in proportion to
 * the number of cores.
 *
 * ## Data races
 *
 * Two isolates can never modify the same object, because no object is
 * ever in two heaps. Data races on Zuri values can't happen, and you
 * don't need locks around your own data structures.
 *
 * When you pass a closure to an isolate, the variables it captures and
 * the globals it refers to are copied when it's spawned. The isolate
 * works on its own copy, so later changes made by the caller don't
 * affect the isolate, and changes the isolate makes never reach the
 * caller.
 *
 * ## Compared with Go
 *
 * Go runs goroutines on a small number of OS threads, and they all
 * share one heap. Zuri runs isolates on a pool of OS threads, gives
 * each one a heap of its own, and copies values between them.
 *
 * | | Go goroutines | Zuri isolates |
 * | :--- | :--- | :--- |
 * | Model | Green threads multiplexed onto OS threads, sharing one heap | A pool of OS threads, with a separate heap for each isolate |
 * | Memory safety | Memory is shared, so data races are possible without locks | Heaps are separate, so data races can't happen |
 * | Garbage collection | One collector for the whole program | A separate collector for each isolate, with no pauses across threads |
 * | Cost per task | About 2 KB of stack per goroutine | A few hundred bytes for the queue entry and handle, with threads reused |
 * | Communication | Channels that pass pointers, or locks | Copied values, or native resources that are moved |
 * | Best at | Very large numbers of concurrent network connections | Parallel computation and task pipelines |
 *
 * ## Values that can be passed between isolates
 *
 * Almost every kind of value can be passed to another isolate:
 *
 * - Primitives: `nil`, `bool`, `number`, `bigint`, `string`, `bytes`
 *   and `range`.
 * - Lists and dictionaries, including nested and cyclic ones.
 * - Class instances, classes, with their static fields, superclasses
 *   and methods, and bound methods.
 * - Functions, closures with their captured variables, anonymous
 *   functions and top-level scripts.
 * - Modules, including a module that a closure captured.
 * - Native handles: `Ptr` handles, which are moved, and `Channel` and
 *   `Isolate` handles.
 *
 * A module isn't copied. It's loaded again in the receiving isolate, so
 * the receiver gets its own instance of the module, with its own
 * top-level state.
 *
 * An open file handle, from `file(...)`, can't be passed to another
 * isolate. Trying to do so raises an `IsolateError`.
 *
 * ## Native resources
 *
 * A `Ptr` wraps a native resource such as a database connection, a
 * socket or a compression stream, and those can't safely be duplicated
 * across threads. A `Ptr` is therefore moved instead of copied:
 *
 * - When a `Ptr` is passed to `spawn()`, returned to `join()` or sent
 *   through a `Channel`, the receiver becomes its owner, and the
 *   sender's handle stops working.
 * - Using a handle that has stopped working raises an error.
 * - `Channel` and `Isolate` handles are different. They're safe to use
 *   from several threads, and any number of isolates can share them.
 *
 * ## Failures
 *
 * An error in one isolate stays in that isolate. It never stops other
 * isolates, damages the pool or crashes the process.
 *
 * - An error that an isolate's function doesn't catch is raised again
 *   as an `IsolateError`, with the original file, line and stack trace,
 *   when you call `join()` or `try_join()`.
 * - If the runtime itself fails inside an isolate, that isolate's
 *   virtual machine is discarded and replaced with a new one, so the
 *   pool keeps its full size.
 * - If an isolate fails and its handle is dropped without ever being
 *   joined, a warning is printed, so that the error isn't lost.
 *
 * ## Cancellation
 *
 * Call `Isolate.cancel()` to cancel an isolate that's running or still
 * queued.
 *
 * - If the isolate is waiting in `Channel.send()`, `Channel.recv()`,
 *   `Isolate.join()`, `select()`, `wait_any()` or `wait_all()`, the
 *   call is interrupted and raises an `IsolateCancelledError`.
 * - Code that's busy computing isn't interrupted. It should check
 *   `isolate.is_cancelled()` from time to time and return when it's
 *   set.
 *
 * ## Structured concurrency
 *
 * Isolates that outlive the code that started them can leave tasks
 * running, resources open and errors unnoticed. `isolate.scope(body)`
 * and its `Scope` handle prevent that:
 *
 * - Every isolate started with `s.spawn()` inside a `scope()` call
 *   finishes before `scope()` returns.
 * - If `body` raises an error, or any of the isolates fails, all the
 *   other isolates in the scope are cancelled, so that they don't keep
 *   working for nothing.
 * - `scope()` waits for the cancelled isolates to finish before it
 *   raises the original error to the caller.
 *
 * ## Examples
 *
 * ### Starting a task
 *
 * ```zuri
 * import isolate
 *
 * def calculate_primes(limit) {
 *   var count = 0
 *   iter var n = 2; n <= limit; n++ {
 *     var is_prime = true
 *     iter var d = 2; d * d <= n; d++ {
 *       if n % d == 0 {
 *         is_prime = false
 *         break
 *       }
 *     }
 *     if is_prime count++
 *   }
 *   return count
 * }
 *
 * var task = isolate.spawn(calculate_primes, 100_000)
 * echo "Primes found: ${task.join()}"
 * ```
 *
 * ### Mapping over a list in parallel
 *
 * ```zuri
 * import isolate
 *
 * def square(n) {
 *   return n * n
 * }
 *
 * var numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]
 * var results = isolate.map(square, numbers)
 * echo "Squares: ${results}"
 * ```
 *
 * ### Structured concurrency with `scope()`
 *
 * ```zuri
 * import isolate
 *
 * var total = isolate.scope(def(s) {
 *   var w1 = s.spawn(def() { return 10 * 2 })
 *   var w2 = s.spawn(def() { return 20 * 2 })
 *   return w1.join() + w2.join()
 * })
 * echo "Total: ${total}"
 * ```
 *
 * ### Publish and subscribe with `Broadcast`
 *
 * ```zuri
 * import isolate
 *
 * var hub = isolate.broadcast()
 * var sub1 = hub.subscribe()
 * var sub2 = hub.subscribe()
 *
 * isolate.spawn(def(c) { echo "Isolate 1: ${c.recv()}" }, sub1)
 * isolate.spawn(def(c) { echo "Isolate 2: ${c.recv()}" }, sub2)
 *
 * hub.send("Hello Subscribers!")
 * hub.close()
 * ```
 *
 * @copyright 2026, Richard Ore and Zuri contributors
 */

import _isolate
import @.error { IsolateError, IsolateTimeoutError, IsolateCancelledError }
import @.channel { Channel, channel, select }
import @.broadcast { Broadcast, broadcast }

/**
 * A handle to an isolate, returned by `spawn()`.
 */
class Isolate {
  var _ptr

  @new(ptr) {
    self._ptr = ptr
  }

  /**
   * Waits until the isolate finishes and returns its result. You can
   * call this more than once. Later calls return the same result
   * straight away.
   *
   * @param number timeout: the number of seconds to wait before giving
   *    up. If it's left out, this waits indefinitely.
   * @returns any
   * @raises IsolateError if the isolate's function raised an error that
   *    it didn't catch
   * @raises IsolateTimeoutError if `timeout` passes first
   * @raises IsolateCancelledError if the calling isolate, not this one,
   *    is cancelled while waiting here
   */
  join(timeout: ?number) {
    var r = _isolate.join(self._ptr, timeout)
    if r[0] == 'pending' {
      raise IsolateTimeoutError('join() timed out after ${timeout} seconds')
    }
    if r[0] == 'cancelled' {
      raise IsolateCancelledError(
        'join() was interrupted: the calling isolate was cancelled',
      )
    }
    if r[0] == 'error' {
      raise IsolateError(r[1])
    }
    return r[1]
  }

  /**
   * Asks the isolate to stop. This has no effect on an isolate that has
   * already finished.
   *
   * If the isolate is waiting in `Channel.send()`, `Channel.recv()`,
   * `Isolate.join()`, `wait_any()`, `wait_all()` or `select()`, that
   * call is interrupted within about 50 milliseconds and raises an
   * `IsolateCancelledError`.
   *
   * Otherwise, cancellation is cooperative, and code that's running
   * isn't interrupted. The isolate's function has to check
   * `isolate.is_cancelled()` and return by itself.
   */
  cancel() {
    _isolate.cancel(self._ptr)
  }

  /**
   * @returns bool: `true` if `cancel()` has been called on this
   *    isolate.
   */
  is_cancelled() {
    return _isolate.is_cancelled(self._ptr)
  }

  /**
   * @returns ?string: the name given via `spawn_named()`, or
   *    `nil` if this isolate was started with plain `spawn()`.
   */
  name() {
    return _isolate.name(self._ptr)
  }

  /**
   * Returns the isolate's result, or `nil` if it hasn't finished yet.
   * This doesn't block.
   *
   * @note An isolate that returns `nil` can't be told apart from one
   *    that's still running. Use `is_done()` to tell the difference.
   * @returns any
   * @raises IsolateError if the isolate's function raised an error that
   *    it didn't catch
   */
  try_join() {
    var r = _isolate.try_join(self._ptr)
    if r[0] == 'pending' {
      return nil
    }
    if r[0] == 'error' {
      raise IsolateError(r[1])
    }
    return r[1]
  }

  /**
   * @returns bool: `true` if the isolate has finished.
   */
  is_done() {
    return _isolate.is_done(self._ptr)
  }

  /**
   * The isolate's current state.
   *
   * Unlike `try_join()`, this never marks a failure as handled. You can
   * call it as often as you like to check on progress, and the warning
   * for a failure that's never passed to `join()` or `try_join()` is
   * still printed. See "Failures" in the module documentation.
   *
   * @returns string: `'pending'` while the isolate is running, `'done'`
   *    once it has finished successfully, or `'error'` once it has
   *    finished with an error it didn't catch
   */
  status() {
    return _isolate.status(self._ptr)
  }

  /**
   * The isolate's native handle. `wait_any()` needs it to work on a
   * plain list of `Isolate` objects from outside the class, and it
   * isn't meant to be used directly.
   */
  handle() {
    return self._ptr
  }
}

def _do_spawn(fn, args, name) {
  var r = _isolate.spawn(fn, args, name)
  if r[0] == 'error' {
    raise IsolateError(r[1])
  }
  return Isolate(r[1])
}

/**
 * Starts running `fn` on the isolate pool and returns a handle to it.
 * The module documentation lists the values that can be passed in `fn`
 * and `args`.
 *
 * For example:
 *
 * ```zuri-repl
 * %> def square(n) { return n * n }
 * %> var t = isolate.spawn(square, 9)
 * %> t.join()
 * 81
 * ```
 *
 * @param function fn: a closure, bound method or class method
 * @param ...any args
 * @returns Isolate
 * @raises TypeError if `fn` can't be called
 * @raises IsolateError if `fn` is a native function or a class, if `fn`
 *    or `args` can't be passed to another isolate, or if the pool has
 *    been shut down with `shutdown()`
 */
def spawn(fn: function, ...args) {
  return _do_spawn(fn, args, nil)
}

/**
 * Works like `spawn()`, but gives the isolate a `name`. The name is
 * only a label for debugging. `Isolate.name()` returns it, and it's
 * included in the warning that's printed if this isolate fails and is
 * never passed to `join()` or `try_join()`. See "Failures" in the
 * module documentation.
 *
 * @param string name
 * @param function fn: a closure, bound method or class method
 * @param ...any args
 * @returns Isolate
 * @raises TypeError if `fn` can't be called
 * @raises IsolateError if `fn` is a native function or a class, if `fn`
 *    or `args` can't be passed to another isolate, or if the pool has
 *    been shut down with `shutdown()`
 */
def spawn_named(name: string, fn: function, ...args) {
  return _do_spawn(fn, args, name)
}

/**
 * Supervises a group of isolates started inside a call to
 * `isolate.scope()`.
 *
 * A `Scope` guarantees three things:
 *
 * 1. Every isolate started with `s.spawn()` or `s.spawn_named()` is
 *    tracked by the scope, and none of them can outlive the
 *    `scope(body)` call.
 * 2. If any of the isolates raises an error that it doesn't catch, or
 *    if the body of the scope fails, all the remaining isolates are
 *    cancelled with `cancel()`, so that they don't keep working for
 *    nothing.
 * 3. `scope()` waits for every isolate, including the cancelled ones,
 *    to finish before it raises the original error or returns the
 *    body's result.
 *
 * You can't create a `Scope` yourself. `isolate.scope(body)` creates
 * one and passes it to `body`.
 *
 * ## Examples
 *
 * ### Combining the results of two tasks
 *
 * ```zuri
 * import isolate
 *
 * var summary = isolate.scope(@(s) {
 *   var task_a = s.spawn(@() { return "Task A complete" })
 *   var task_b = s.spawn(@() { return "Task B complete" })
 *   return "${task_a.join()} & ${task_b.join()}"
 * })
 * echo summary # "Task A complete & Task B complete"
 * ```
 *
 * ### Cancelling the other tasks when one fails
 *
 * ```zuri
 * import isolate
 *
 * catch {
 *   isolate.scope(def(s) {
 *     # This isolate fails straight away.
 *     s.spawn(def() {
 *       raise "critical error in child"
 *     })
 *     # This one is cancelled as soon as the first one fails.
 *     s.spawn(def() {
 *       iter var i = 0; i < 1_000_000; i++ {
 *         if isolate.is_cancelled() break
 *       }
 *     })
 *   })
 * } as err {
 *   echo "Scope caught child failure: ${err.message}"
 * }
 * ```
 */
class Scope {
  var _children

  @new() {
    self._children = []
  }

  /**
   * Starts an isolate that this scope keeps track of.
   *
   * The isolate is tied to the scope. `isolate.scope()` doesn't return
   * until the isolate has finished, and if the isolate fails, all the
   * other isolates in the scope are cancelled.
   *
   * @param function fn: the function, closure or method to run
   * @param ...any args: the arguments to pass to `fn`
   * @returns Isolate: a handle to the new isolate
   * @raises TypeError if `fn` can't be called
   * @raises IsolateError if `fn` is a native function or a class, if
   *    `fn` or `args` can't be passed to another isolate, or if the
   *    pool has been shut down
   *
   * ```zuri
   * isolate.scope(def(s) {
   *   var w = s.spawn(def(x, y) { return x + y }, 10, 20)
   *   echo "Result: ${w.join()}" # 30
   * })
   * ```
   */
  spawn(fn: function, ...args) {
    var t = _do_spawn(fn, args, nil)
    self._children.append(t)
    return t
  }

  /**
   * Starts a named isolate that this scope keeps track of.
   *
   * This works like `Scope.spawn()`, but gives the isolate a `name`,
   * which appears in error messages and helps with debugging.
   *
   * @param string name: a name for the isolate, for people to read
   * @param function fn: the function, closure or method to run
   * @param ...any args: the arguments to pass to `fn`
   * @returns Isolate: a handle to the new isolate
   * @raises TypeError if `fn` can't be called
   * @raises IsolateError if `fn` is a native function or a class, if
   *    `fn` or `args` can't be passed to another isolate, or if the
   *    pool has been shut down
   *
   * ```zuri
   * isolate.scope(def(s) {
   *   var w = s.spawn_named("fetch-user", fetch_user, user_id)
   *   return w.join()
   * })
   * ```
   */
  spawn_named(name: string, fn: function, ...args) {
    var t = _do_spawn(fn, args, name)
    self._children.append(t)
    return t
  }

  /**
   * All the isolates started in this scope.
   *
   * @returns list: the `Isolate` handles, in the order they were
   *    started
   *
   * ```zuri
   * isolate.scope(def(s) {
   *   s.spawn(task1)
   *   s.spawn(task2)
   *
   *   for child in s.children() {
   *     echo "Child isolate active"
   *   }
   * })
   * ```
   */
  children() {
    return self._children
  }
}

/**
 * Runs `body(s)` inside a structured concurrency scope.
 *
 * Every isolate started with `s.spawn()` or `s.spawn_named()` finishes
 * before `scope()` returns.
 *
 * ## Errors and cancellation
 *
 * 1. `scope()` waits until `body(s)` returns and every isolate in the
 *    scope has finished.
 * 2. If `body` raises an error, or any isolate in the scope fails, all
 *    the isolates that are still running are cancelled with `cancel()`.
 * 3. `scope()` waits for the cancelled isolates to finish before it
 *    returns or raises an error.
 * 4. If `body` raised an error, that error is raised again. Otherwise,
 *    the first failure among the isolates is raised, not counting the
 *    `IsolateCancelledError` errors that the cancellation caused.
 *
 * @param function body: a function, `def(s)`, that receives a new
 *    `Scope`
 * @returns any: whatever `body(s)` returns
 * @raises IsolateError if an isolate in the scope failed and `body`
 *    didn't raise an error
 * @raises any: any error raised by `body(s)`
 *
 * ```zuri
 * import isolate
 *
 * var result = isolate.scope(def(s) {
 *   var task1 = s.spawn(def() { return 100 })
 *   var task2 = s.spawn(def() { return 200 })
 *   return task1.join() + task2.join()
 * })
 * echo "Total: ${result}" # 300
 * ```
 */
def scope(body: function) {
  var s = Scope()
  var body_error = nil
  var result = nil
  catch {
    result = body(s)
  } as e {
    body_error = e
  }

  # cancel every child regardless of outcome; a no-op on ones that
  # already finished, and lets still-running ones wind down sooner
  # once `body` (or a sibling) has already failed.
  for c in s.children() {
    c.cancel()
  }

  # A child that was blocked in a cancellation-aware call (`recv()`,
  # `join()`, ...) when the sweep above cancelled it fails with a
  # message that starts with the *original* raised class's name --
  # `join()` here only ever wraps it fresh as `IsolateError`, so
  # that's the one place the distinction survives the trip back from
  # the child's own isolate. Skipping it keeps `first_failure` pointed
  # at whatever ACTUALLY caused the cancellation sweep, rather than a
  # downstream side effect of it.
  var first_failure = nil
  for c in s.children() {
    catch {
      c.join()
    } as e {
      if first_failure == nil and
          !e.message.starts_with('IsolateCancelledError:') {
        first_failure = e
      }
    }
  }

  if body_error != nil {
    raise body_error
  }
  if first_failure != nil {
    raise first_failure
  }
  return result
}

/**
 * Sets the largest number of threads that the pool can run at once.
 * This has no effect once the pool has started, because its size is
 * fixed as soon as the first isolate runs.
 *
 * The size is a maximum. A thread is only started when an isolate is
 * waiting and every thread that's already running is busy, so a pool
 * sized for peak load only uses as many threads as the load actually
 * needs.
 *
 * @param int threads
 * @returns bool: `true` if the size was set, or `false` if the pool had
 *    already started
 * @raises ValueError if `threads` is less than 1
 */
def configure(threads: int) {
  if threads < 1 {
    raise ValueError(
      'configure() expects threads to be at least 1, got ${threads}',
    )
  }
  return _isolate.configure(threads)
}

/**
 * The largest number of threads that the pool can run at once. If the
 * pool hasn't started yet, this starts it, with the size set by
 * `configure()`, or with one thread per CPU if `configure()` wasn't
 * called.
 *
 * @returns int
 */
def pool_size() {
  return _isolate.pool_size()
}

/**
 * The number of threads the pool has started so far. Threads are
 * started as isolates need them, so this number follows the highest
 * number of isolates that have been waiting at the same time, and it
 * never goes above `pool_size()`.
 *
 * @returns int
 */
def started_count() {
  return _isolate.started_count()
}

/**
 * @returns int: the number of logical CPUs this machine reports.
 */
def cpu_count() {
  return _isolate.cpu_count()
}

/**
 * Stops the pool from accepting any more `spawn()` calls, then waits
 * until every isolate that has already been spawned, whether it's still
 * queued or already running, has finished. Nothing that's in progress
 * is abandoned.
 *
 * This is permanent. After it's called, every `spawn()` for the rest of
 * the process raises an `IsolateError`, even if this call later times
 * out.
 *
 * @param number timeout: the number of seconds to wait before giving
 *    up. If it's left out, this waits indefinitely.
 * @raises IsolateTimeoutError if `timeout` passes while isolates are
 *    still running
 */
def shutdown(timeout: ?number) {
  if !_isolate.shutdown(timeout) {
    raise IsolateTimeoutError('shutdown() timed out after ${timeout} seconds')
  }
}

/**
 * @returns int: the number of isolates that are running right now.
 *    Isolates still waiting in the queue, which `queued_count()`
 *    counts, aren't included.
 */
def active_count() {
  return _isolate.active_count()
}

/**
 * @returns int: the number of isolates that have been spawned but
 *    haven't started running yet
 */
def queued_count() {
  return _isolate.queued_count()
}

/**
 * @returns bool: `true` if `shutdown()` has been called.
 */
def is_shutdown() {
  return _isolate.is_shutdown()
}

/**
 * Waits until at least one of `isolates` finishes, and returns it. Use
 * this to respond to whichever of several tasks finishes first, instead
 * of joining them one at a time in a fixed order.
 *
 * @param [Isolate] isolates: the isolates to wait for. The list must
 *    not be empty.
 * @param number timeout: the number of seconds to wait before giving
 *    up. If it's left out, this waits indefinitely.
 * @returns Isolate: the one that finished first
 * @raises ValueError if `isolates` is empty
 * @raises IsolateTimeoutError if `timeout` passes before any of them
 *    finishes
 * @raises IsolateCancelledError if the calling isolate is cancelled
 *    while waiting here
 */
def wait_any(isolates: list, timeout: ?number) {
  if isolates.length() == 0 {
    raise ValueError('wait_any() expects at least one isolate')
  }
  var ptrs = []
  for c in isolates {
    ptrs.append(c.handle())
  }
  var r = _isolate.wait_any(ptrs, timeout)
  if r[0] == 'timeout' {
    raise IsolateTimeoutError('wait_any() timed out after ${timeout} seconds')
  }
  if r[0] == 'cancelled' {
    raise IsolateCancelledError(
      'wait_any() was interrupted: the calling isolate was cancelled',
    )
  }
  return isolates[r[1]]
}

/**
 * Waits until every one of `isolates` has finished, then returns their
 * results, in the same order as `isolates`.
 *
 * @param [Isolate] isolates: the isolates to wait for. If the list is
 *    empty, this returns `[]`.
 * @param number timeout: the number of seconds to wait for all of them
 *    together, not for each one. If it's left out, this waits
 *    indefinitely.
 * @returns list
 * @raises IsolateError from the first isolate in the list whose
 *    function raised an error, once all of them have finished
 * @raises IsolateTimeoutError if `timeout` passes before all of them
 *    have finished
 * @raises IsolateCancelledError if the calling isolate is cancelled
 *    while waiting here
 */
def wait_all(isolates: list, timeout: ?number) {
  if isolates.length() == 0 {
    return []
  }
  var ptrs = []
  for c in isolates {
    ptrs.append(c.handle())
  }
  var status = _isolate.wait_all(ptrs, timeout)
  if status == 'timeout' {
    raise IsolateTimeoutError('wait_all() timed out after ${timeout} seconds')
  }
  if status == 'cancelled' {
    raise IsolateCancelledError(
      'wait_all() was interrupted: the calling isolate was cancelled',
    )
  }
  var results = []
  for c in isolates {
    results.append(c.join())
  }
  return results
}

/**
 * Calls `fn` once for each item in `items`, in parallel on the isolate
 * pool, and returns the results in the same order as `items`. This is
 * the same as spawning one isolate per item and passing the handles to
 * `wait_all()`.
 *
 * @param function fn: a closure, bound method or class method
 * @param list items
 * @param number timeout: the number of seconds to wait for the whole
 *    batch. If it's left out, this waits indefinitely.
 * @returns list
 * @raises IsolateError from the first item in the list whose call
 *    raised an error, once every call has finished
 * @raises IsolateTimeoutError if `timeout` passes before every call has
 *    finished
 * @raises IsolateCancelledError if the calling isolate is cancelled
 *    while waiting here
 */
def map(fn: function, items: list, timeout: ?number) {
  if items.length() == 0 {
    return []
  }
  var r = _isolate.map(fn, items, timeout)
  if r[0] == 'timeout' {
    raise IsolateTimeoutError('map() timed out after ${timeout} seconds')
  }
  if r[0] == 'cancelled' {
    raise IsolateCancelledError(
      'map() was interrupted: the calling isolate was cancelled',
    )
  }
  if r[0] == 'error' {
    raise IsolateError(r[1])
  }
  return r[1]
}

/**
 * Whether the isolate that's running this code has been asked to stop
 * with `cancel()`. Long-running code, and loops in particular, should
 * check this from time to time so that they can return early.
 *
 * Outside an isolate, in the main script or on another thread, this
 * always returns `false`.
 *
 * @returns bool
 */
def is_cancelled() {
  return _isolate.current_is_cancelled()
}
